.TH VIBEOS-SANDBOX 1 "2026-11-17" "vibeos 0.1.1" "VibeOS"
.SH NAME
vibeos-sandbox \- runs the apps installed from Software in a sandbox, with the permissions the user gave them
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vibeos-sandbox run
.RB [ \-\-file\-forwarding ]
.I APP-ID
.B \-\-
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARG ...]
.br
.B vibeos-sandbox add
.RB [ \-\-origin=store | file ]
.I PACKAGE
\&...
.br
.B vibeos-sandbox remove
.I PACKAGE
\&...
.br
.B vibeos-sandbox sync
.RB [ \-\-wait ]
.RB [ \-\-history\-client=\fITEXT\fR ]
.br
.B vibeos-sandbox list
.br
.B vibeos-sandbox permissions
.I APP-ID
.br
.B vibeos-sandbox set
.I APP-ID PERMISSION
.BR allow | ask | deny
.br
.B vibeos-sandbox clear-cache
.I PACKAGE
.br
.B vibeos-sandbox ps
.br
.B vibeos-sandbox kill
.I APP-ID
.SH DESCRIPTION
The apps installed from Software, or from a .deb file with App Installer, run sandboxed. VibeOS's own apps do not.
.PP
.B run
starts
.I COMMAND
as the app
.I APP-ID
in a sandbox made with bubblewrap: the system's
.IR /usr ,
.I /etc
and
.I /opt
read-only, a home of its own where the user's home is
.RI ( ~/.local/share/vibeos-sandbox/data/ PACKAGE ),
its own
.I /tmp
and processes, the compositor's, PipeWire's and PulseAudio's sockets, the GPU, and a session bus filtered by
xdg-dbus-proxy (in a bubblewrap of its own). It never sees the system bus, the sound cards or the input devices.
A /.flatpak-info names the app, so the portals (the file chooser, the location, the camera, notifications, running
in the background), PipeWire and GNOME Shell treat it as they treat a Flatpak app. With
.BR \-\-file\-forwarding ,
the files and URIs between
.B @@
(or
.BR @@u )
and
.B @@
in the command are the ones the user opened with the app: they go to it through the document portal.
.B vibeos-sandbox
stays outside as the sandbox's supervisor and takes everything away when the app ends.
.PP
.B add
sandboxes the apps of the installed packages: for each app, a desktop file in
.I ~/.local/share/applications
that starts it with
.BR "vibeos-sandbox run" ,
the same for its D-Bus services in
.IR ~/.local/share/dbus-1/services ;
its autostart entries are hidden in
.I ~/.config/autostart
unless the app may run in the background, and its systemd user units are masked. The package's own files are never
changed. A file of the user's own in the way is kept beside it, as
.IR NAME.vibeos-sandbox-saved ,
and put back by
.BR remove .
An app whose desktop id is not one the portals accept (firefox-esr) shows as
.RI vibeos.app. ID .
App Installer runs
.B add
for what it installs new from Software or from a .deb file.
.B remove
stops sandboxing a package; its files in the sandbox stay.
.PP
.B sync
follows what dpkg did: a sandboxed package that was updated gets its files again; one that was uninstalled (removed or
purged) loses them, and its apps lose what they kept, as on Android: their home in
.I ~/.local/share/vibeos-sandbox/data/
(their files and settings) and their entries in the permission store, the documents they were given among them. A
package that dpkg is still unpacking, configuring or removing is left as it is;
.B \-\-wait
waits until dpkg's status has stayed the same for two seconds, and looks again while dpkg wrote it meanwhile or left
a package half done, for up to ten minutes. The first time, it also sandboxes the packages that
PackageKit's history says App Installer installed before;
.B \-\-history\-client
looks for another program in the history instead, every time it is given. A systemd user unit runs it as the session
starts, and another one, with
.BR \-\-wait ,
whenever dpkg's status changes.
.PP
.B clear-cache
deletes what a sandboxed package's apps keep in their cache (the
.I .cache
folder in their home, their XDG_CACHE_HOME); they make it again as they need it. Settings' "Clear cache" runs it.
.SH PERMISSIONS
.B permissions
lists what the app may do, and whether it asks for it;
.B set
changes it, and taking a permission away stops the app at once. They are kept in the desktop's permission store
(xdg-permission-store), where the portals read them too.
.PP
An app asks for permissions in its desktop file, with the names below:
.B X-VibeOS-Permissions=network;camera;
\&. One it does not name is never allowed, whatever the store says, and
.B set
refuses it; an app whose desktop file has no such key may be given any of them. The permissions:
.TP
.B network
allowed by default (to an app that names its permissions, when it names this one); without it the app has a
network of its own with nothing in it.
.TP
.B location
the location portal asks the first time (\fBask\fR); allowed or not, from then on.
.TP
.B camera
the camera portal asks the first time; allowed, the app also gets the camera devices.
.TP
.B microphone
not allowed by default: WirePlumber hides the microphones from the app, and does not link what it records.
.TP
.BR contacts ", " calendar
not allowed by default: evolution-data-server's address books, or calendars, on the filtered bus.
.TP
.BR phone ", " sms
not allowed by default: the dialer's call service, the messaging app's SMS service.
.TP
.B files
not allowed by default: the shared folders (Documents, Downloads, Music, Pictures, Videos...) and removable media.
Without it the app gets only the files the user picks in the file chooser or opens with it.
.TP
.B background
an app with no windows left is asked about (\fBask\fR); allowed, its autostart entries run too.
.PP
A permission changed takes effect at the app's next start, except the location, the camera, the microphone and
running in the background, which take effect at once.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/vibeos-sandbox/packages/
one record per sandboxed package: its apps and the files written for them.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/vibeos-sandbox/data/
one home per sandboxed package; deleted when the package is uninstalled.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/.flatpak/
one directory per running sandbox, as Flatpak's, for the portals.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bwrap (1),
.BR xdg-dbus-proxy (1),
.BR vibeos-installer (1)
